# Worktrees: a second checkout of this repository, made by whichever tool is
# running parallel work. Never part of a commit -- a worktree is a checkout, not
# content, and `git add -A` in the main checkout would otherwise stage a whole
# second tree of the files that are already tracked.
#
# Every place one is created, at any depth. `.claude/worktrees/` is where this
# repo's agents put them and is covered by the first pattern, which is
# deliberately not anchored: a named exception for it beside that pattern would
# be the same rule written twice. `.worktrees/` is the other spelling the
# tooling in use reaches for, and `<name>.worktree/` is the shape a tool that
# makes one per branch leaves behind. Git's own registration lives under `.git/`,
# which is never committed in the first place; this is about the working copy.
worktrees/
.worktrees/
*.worktree/

# Codewhale runtime scratch: lock files, the agent harness's own journal and
# state, and pasted attachments. Tracked repo content under .codewhale/
# (instructions, skills) must never land here.
.codewhale/harness/
.codewhale/pastes/
.codewhale/state/
.codewhale/workflow-runs.jsonl

